{{also|Haven}}
==English==

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|haven}}, {{m|enm|havene}}, from {{inh|en|ang|hæfen||haven; harbour; port}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*habanu}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*habnō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*habanō}} (compare {{cog|nl|haven}}, {{cog|de|Hafen}}, {{cog|no|-}}/{{cog|da|havn}}, {{cog|sv|hamn}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*habą||sea}} (compare {{cog|ang|hæf}}, {{cog|gml|haf}}, {{cog|non|haf||sea}}, {{cog|de|Haff||bay or lagoon behind a spit}}, perhaps, in the sense of "heaving sea", etymologically identical with {{cog|non|haf||heaving, lifting, uplift, elevation}}, derived from {{cog|gem-pro|*habjaną||to lift, heave}}), or from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*kh₂pnós}} (compare {{cog|sga|cúan||harbor, recess, haven}}). {{doublet|en|abra}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈheɪvən/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-haven.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪvən|s=2}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|slightly|dated}} A [[harbour]] or [[anchorage]] protected from the sea.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Pericles|13|What ſhipping, and what ladings in our '''hauen''',}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|II|"Break, break, break,"|229|And the stately ships go on / To their '''haven''' under the hill;}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A place of [[safety]].
#: {{syn|en|refuge|sanctuary|zoar}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 21, 2011|author=Helen Pidd| title=Europeans migrate south as continent drifts deeper into crisis| work=the Guardian| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/21/europe-migrants-crisis-irish-portuguese| passage=Since its conception, the European Union has been a '''haven''' for those seeking refuge from war, persecution and poverty in other parts of the world.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A peaceful place.

